hen with the invasion going into its  second week and into a phase whereby the  russian advances the russian attacks on  civilian areas according to the  ukrainians and what is seems to be  on the ground an investigation in in all  directions including the international  criminal court  there is  very very little in the way of common  ground between the two sides the the  biggest hope of all that ukraine has is  that there are humanitarian corridors  formed  not for the sort of purpose that  the president putin was was suggesting  but for the purpose of getting the  injured civilians the injured out of  this country and aid into this country  that is what that that is what president  zlensky wants and he's in a pretty  desperate position it would seem even  though his his fighters are are doing  incredibly well in many areas but as he  said  thousands of them are dying  and what's the latest andrew on the  humanitarian situation there in  the various cities of ukraine which  are under attack  well i'm looking here at just so many  reports coming in uh  in hersen of course this is the first  city in ukraine to fall to the russians  there are no reports of any further  fighting but it's understood that the  ukrainian forces have withdrawn and  there is an intent on counter-attacks  all over the country where losses have  been made but no further word from there  and chernoheave in the north  just near the border with belarus  there has been a number of attacks a  diesel plant  is on fire and there's been air strikes  on  reportedly two schools  we're hearing now from the emergency  service there  that 22 civilians have been  killed  in the attacks and there are  rescue workers are searching for more  bodies and and trying to rescue people  from the rubble um elsewhere in haki the  situation is absolutely critical um one  official in the presidential office has  described it as a 21st century  stalingrad uh that city  and then we have  uh in the  in the port of mariopol uh which which  is in uh the sea of azeroth the sea of  azov port  massive siege there uh people just  cannot break out and they're they've run  out of all supplies food water uh and  fuel uh critical situation uh appeals  for  a host of  areas of help particularly medical help  because the hospitals  are in a critical state unable to treat  people properly this is a real emergency  of a profound sort um in the capital  heave  there have been further attacks but  there it's just a  an eerie weight for what people feel is  inevitable and that's the push from so  much in the way of armoured columns uh  to come into the city uh there were four  explosions uh uh 12 hours ago sorry 18  hours ago but  there we don't know about the number of  injuries but certainly were very loud  loud explosions overnight and overall  the situation  uh in ukraine right now  is incredibly gloomy  i'll just hear us andrew simmons  reporting from levitt in western ukraine  andrew many thanks indeed

